WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.210
There's a
lot of messed up prisons around the world.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.838
But I found this one
to be the most disturbing.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:08.008
It's a fictional prison
from the show ANDOR and it's designed

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:11.511
specifically for convicts
that are says to be labor worthy.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:15.849
It can hold up to 5000 inmates
and they're grouped into these work teams.

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:20.895
And day after day, they work together
to assemble these mechanical parts.

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:23.148
But no one actually knows
what they're building.

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:26.026
The genius part about this prison
is that the control

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:29.863
mechanism is built directly
into the architecture.

00:00:29.946 --> 00:00:35.285
For example, thes tungsten steel floors
can fry you and it can even kill you.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.454
It's also designed to make the prisoners

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:39.622
think that they're
being constantly watched.

00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:44.210
And this idea of constant surveillance
makes them incredibly obedient.

00:00:44.294 --> 00:00:47.172
And as we studied prisons more and more

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:50.592
a very disturbing idea started to pop up.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:54.262
The purpose of prisons is to detain
people.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:57.682
Philosophically, it gets a little blurry
when you start thinking about, you know,

00:00:57.682 --> 00:01:01.352
if the purpose is to punish
or to rehabilitate.

00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:05.565
But functionally, it's a place
meant to contain prisoners

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:07.984
and to keep the rest of society safe.

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:10.904
But what happens
when the architecture itself

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:14.866
becomes the surveillor,
the detainee and the Punisher?

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:19.746
A portion of this video
is sponsored by Milanote.

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:21.831
This is the Panopticon.

00:01:21.831 --> 00:01:23.750
It's an architectural concept for prisons

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.504
from the 18th century
by the philosopher Jeremy Bentham,

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:31.758
The word panopticon derives
from the Greek word for all seeing.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.636
And it's designed
around a central watchtower

00:01:34.636 --> 00:01:37.388
where the guards
can always have a view of the prisoners.

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:39.599
But the prisoners can't see the guards.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:43.269
This creates the illusion
of being constantly watched, meaning

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:47.107
there's a higher chance the prisoners
are going to behave and self-regulate.

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:50.735
And the entire facility
can be monitored by just a few guards.

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:52.779
So it's not just physical containment.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:55.365
It's kind of a psychological control.

00:01:55.365 --> 00:01:57.742
And this complete erosion of privacy

00:01:57.742 --> 00:02:01.871
can lead to anxiety, paranoia
and a loss of dignity.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.624
Although this exact design
was never implemented,

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.752
we've seen variations of this concept
all over the world.

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:09.879
Most of these prisons were shut down.

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:13.800
But I think this architectural typology
solidified a broader

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.554
social message
and how society perceives prisoners.

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:19.139
It suggests that surveillance

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:23.434
and control are essential in managing
criminal behavior, and it led us down

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:33.403
a path that focused on punishment
rather than rehabilitation.

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:36.781
This is the sound of solitary confinement.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.784
This is where they send prisoners
who are considered to be a threat

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:42.787
to security or other inmates.

00:02:42.954 --> 00:02:44.289
It's an empty room.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:47.292
And the average size
something around six by nine feet.

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:51.379
It's mostly windowless
without any views or sunlight.

00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:54.340
Prisoners spend up to 23 hours at a time,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.178
and for one or 2 hours a day, they're
let out into another isolated area.

00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:02.348
And when you listen to stories of inmates
who spent time there, what's amazing

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.686
is just how quickly
their mental state starts to deteriorate.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:10.064
And to understand why,
let's take a look at another

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:13.276
type of detainment.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:15.236
Everything in the room is white,

00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:18.656
including the floors,
beds, clothing, lights.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.534
And even the food is white.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:24.495
The rooms are completely soundproof
and the lights

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:27.498
are designed
to remove any kind of shadows.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.460
It's called the White Room torture.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:35.423
And it's a form of psycho logical torture
using complete sensory deprivation.

00:03:35.465 --> 00:03:38.927
And it can leave permanent
psychological damage.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.512
One political prisoner
who endured white torture

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:45.475
for eight months couldn't
even remember the faces of his parents.

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:45.892
And that's

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:49.687
because a normal brain needs interaction
and activity for the cells to grow.

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:54.108
And studies show that when you remove
stimulation, the brain freaks out.

00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:56.653
And it can cause anxiety and depression.

00:03:56.653 --> 00:03:58.363
And because the brain is constantly

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:02.575
trying to establish patterns,
it can also lead to hallucinations.

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:07.205
In fact, just 15 minutes of sensory
deprivation can lead to anhedonia,

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:10.667
which is the complete inability
to feel pleasure.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:15.588
And of course, solitary confinement is
not as controlled as a white room torture.

00:04:15.797 --> 00:04:19.259
But there's just so many parallels
that it's kind of hard to ignore.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.054
Solitary confinement
can also leave permanent psychological

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:24.847
and physical damage.

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:28.601
And despite the backlash
from multiple human rights organizations,

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.311
it's still a very common

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:34.065
practice in prisons throughout the world,
especially in the U.S..

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:37.068
In all of these cases,
what's really interesting is that

00:04:37.068 --> 00:04:40.571
architecture is not used
just for physical containment

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:45.243
or a functional necessity,
but it's use for psychological dominance.

00:04:45.493 --> 00:04:46.494
One, two.

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:51.207
It seems to have an 818 and you feel it

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:53.418
for 814.

00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:54.794
Otherwise, a714.

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:59.465
And I think this is where the dilemma is,
especially for architects.

00:04:59.549 --> 00:05:03.177
Architecture, at its core
is supposed to serve the public.

00:05:03.261 --> 00:05:07.932
It's actually ingrained in the profession
and it's even in the air code of ethics.

00:05:08.016 --> 00:05:11.769
Architects must uphold human rights
in all their professional endeavors

00:05:11.769 --> 00:05:13.604
and make the built environment accessible

00:05:13.604 --> 00:05:17.442
to all and respect
the dignity of all people.

00:05:17.525 --> 00:05:21.529
Yes, we need prisons to protect
the broader public from criminals.

00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:25.033
But the line becomes kind of blurry
when the architecture is designed

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:28.536
specifically
for the purpose of causing harm to another

00:05:28.536 --> 00:05:33.374
human being.

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:36.586
If you've been following this channel,
you probably know that we use Milanote.

