00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:27.303
This is considered to be one

00:06:27.303 --> 00:06:30.306
of the most effective prisons
in the world.

00:06:30.431 --> 00:06:32.558
The Halden Prison in Norway.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:36.521
And when I say effective, I'm
talking about the recidivism rate,

00:06:36.604 --> 00:06:40.024
which is the tendency for a convict
to re-offend.

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:44.612
Halden has a rate of 20%
compared to the 76%,

00:06:44.612 --> 00:06:47.323
which is the average
for prisons in the US.

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:49.575
And of course, there's
a whole lot of factors

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:52.412
outside the prison environment
that impact this number.

00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:54.705
And many prisons in the U.S.

00:06:54.705 --> 00:06:58.543
are actually operated by private companies
who make a profit

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:00.253
from the prison population.

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:04.173
So maybe there is an incentive
to keep them in the system.

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:08.136
But Halden success is the result of

00:07:08.136 --> 00:07:11.139
actually a pretty simple idea.

00:07:11.222 --> 00:07:16.519
Normality, meaning the prison should
resemble life outside as much as possible.

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:20.189
They have workshops, music and recording
studios, a worship room.

00:07:20.398 --> 00:07:23.151
They even have a place
where your family can stay overnight.

00:07:23.151 --> 00:07:27.405
As of 2012, Halden had 340 staff members

00:07:27.488 --> 00:07:30.491
for only 228 inmates.

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.244
That's more than one per prisoner.

00:07:33.244 --> 00:07:36.831
Kind of the opposite of the idea
of the efficient automated control

00:07:36.831 --> 00:07:40.585
that we see in Narkina 5.

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:43.963
But this prison is in Norway.

00:07:44.046 --> 00:07:46.591
So is it even a fair comparison?

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:50.595
Norway has about a 1/60
of the population of the U.S.

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:55.433
The incarceration rate is about 75
per 100,000 people.

00:07:55.475 --> 00:07:59.687
Within the U.S.,
it's about 700 per 100,000,

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:03.941
which is about 9.3 times
higher than Norway.

00:08:04.192 --> 00:08:08.529
This also means Norway
can spend a lot more money per prisoner.

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:14.243
Norway spends around $127,000 per prisoner
a year,

00:08:14.494 --> 00:08:18.498
compared to just $25,000 in the US.

00:08:18.581 --> 00:08:24.504
But actually it wasn't always like this.

00:08:24.587 --> 00:08:25.963
Before the nineties,

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:29.634
Norway also had a recidivism rate
similar to the U.S.

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:33.262
70% and a rising prison population.

00:08:33.471 --> 00:08:34.514
And in fact it was

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:38.059
because they found these to be
so not cost effective

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:41.354
in the long run that they took a hard
look at their approach.

00:08:41.562 --> 00:08:45.107
During this time, there was also more
and more research coming out that talked

00:08:45.107 --> 00:08:49.946
about the limitations of prison models
that are focused primarily on punishment.

00:08:49.987 --> 00:08:53.574
Also, there was just more awareness
around human rights issues

00:08:53.574 --> 00:08:56.577
and more awareness
around the treatment of prisoners.

00:08:56.744 --> 00:09:00.540
And the people actually start
to push for something more ethical.

00:09:00.623 --> 00:09:04.293
And so all of this led
to some major changes in their approach,

00:09:04.293 --> 00:09:08.047
where they went from a punitive model
to a rehabilitation model.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:11.217
And that actually had a huge impact
on the architecture.

00:09:11.425 --> 00:09:14.887
Most of these prisons fall
into one of these categories circular

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:18.432
or the panopticon
layout with a central watchtower and salt

00:09:18.432 --> 00:09:21.519
along the perimeter courtyard
layout with the structure

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:26.482
encircling a central open area, triangular
layout with triangular structures

00:09:26.482 --> 00:09:30.403
connected by a spine linear
layout with rows of buildings

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:35.783
stacked along a central spine
x shaped layout with a cross shaped form

00:09:35.825 --> 00:09:40.538
and the radial layout with branches,
they come off a central point.

00:09:40.663 --> 00:09:43.708
Apparently they'd rotate the prisoners
to these different arms

00:09:43.708 --> 00:09:47.336
so they can never familiarize themselves
with where they are

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.590
and the prison and Andor
would fall into this category.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:54.260
But with the sleeping quarters
at the perimeter, by consolidating

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:57.555
all the facilities
into a single continuous building,

00:09:57.763 --> 00:10:01.559
it maximizes efficiency
because you can limit the circulation to

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:03.144
just a few hallways,

00:10:03.144 --> 00:10:06.939
which means you can be a lot
more efficient with your surveillance.

00:10:07.023 --> 00:10:08.107
In Narkina 5.

00:10:08.107 --> 00:10:11.611
This is taken to the street
and even the cells are stripped

00:10:11.611 --> 00:10:16.365
to the bare essentials and designed
for maximum efficiency and control.

00:10:16.449 --> 00:10:19.744
All the basic amenities
like beds, toilets, food,

00:10:19.744 --> 00:10:22.913
water are neatly compact in the cell.

00:10:22.997 --> 00:10:26.334
This unit gives you access
to unlimited bland food,

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.630
but if you're productive enough,
you can be rewarded with flavor.

00:10:30.713 --> 00:10:35.092
There's a screen that displays the amount
of days that's left in your sentence.

00:10:35.092 --> 00:10:38.471
And while there aren't any doors
there's sensors

00:10:38.471 --> 00:10:41.932
that ensure that there's only one person
in a cell.

00:10:42.016 --> 00:10:44.226
Otherwise, you'll get fried.

00:10:44.226 --> 00:10:47.855
The sanitation areas are right
before you enter the work room.

00:10:48.022 --> 00:10:50.900
And all of this is to limit
the amount of movement

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:57.448
from work directly to your cells.

00:10:57.531 --> 00:11:00.701
But in
this new model, the facilities are split

00:11:00.701 --> 00:11:05.164
between the smaller buildings,
surrounded by a natural landscape.

00:11:05.206 --> 00:11:09.919
All of this is contained in this perimeter
wall, kind of like a campus.

00:11:10.002 --> 00:11:13.964
This means they can move around rather
than being restricted to the same hallways

00:11:13.964 --> 00:11:15.091
day after day.

00:11:15.091 --> 00:11:17.468
But what does that mean for surveillance?

00:11:17.468 --> 00:11:21.138
Historically, security
was achieved through the architecture,

00:11:21.222 --> 00:11:23.933
through the spatial layout,
with windowless walls,

00:11:23.933 --> 00:11:26.977
locks, fences,
even cameras and CC systems.

00:11:27.186 --> 00:11:29.021
Let's call this static security.

00:11:29.021 --> 00:11:33.693
But it usually comes at the expense of
creating spaces that are kind of inhumane.

00:11:33.776 --> 00:11:37.446
Narkina 5 prison
while is a very technologically advanced.

00:11:37.613 --> 00:11:40.783
It still operates
on the principles of static security,

00:11:40.991 --> 00:11:46.831
which allows them to have this insane
1: 100 guard to prisoner ratio.

00:11:46.872 --> 00:11:51.794
But despite all the rigorous physical
and psychological control mechanisms,

00:11:52.002 --> 00:11:56.173
static security failed to spot
the communication between the prisoners.

00:11:56.382 --> 00:12:00.177
And this was potentially an oversight
on the architect

00:12:00.261 --> 00:12:05.641
who maybe tried to add a little bit
too much humanity to this prison.

00:12:05.725 --> 00:12:09.937
Technically, if we're strictly abiding
by the principles of static security,

00:12:10.146 --> 00:12:13.107
these windows are not necessary.

00:12:13.107 --> 00:12:16.152
In fact,
it became the fatal flaw of this design

00:12:16.193 --> 00:12:20.823
because allowing them to look outside
the inmates could get a sense of the scale

00:12:20.823 --> 00:12:24.577
of the prison and understand
just how many of them there were

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:28.914
and maybe even start
to communicate with each other.

00:12:28.998 --> 00:12:32.543
So without a design,
that's 100% bulletproof.

00:12:32.626 --> 00:12:35.838
Static
security has some major limitations,

00:12:35.921 --> 00:12:42.178
especially because it can antagonize
and potentially provoke the inmates.

00:12:42.261 --> 00:12:42.595
But in

00:12:42.595 --> 00:12:43.679
Halden and in the

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:47.683
other humane prisons that we looked at,
they've come up with a layer of security

00:12:47.683 --> 00:12:51.937
that's invisible, that allows them
to reduce the amount of static security.

00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:54.523
At Halden they call this dynamic security.

00:12:54.523 --> 00:12:58.277
But essentially it's the relationship
between the inmates and the staff

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.906
created intentionally
through workshops and activities.

00:13:01.989 --> 00:13:05.075
But it only works if we flip the rule

00:13:05.075 --> 00:13:08.704
of the architecture
from a surveiller to a facilitator

00:13:08.788 --> 00:13:12.166
by breaking up the massing
and creating a diversity of spaces.

00:13:12.374 --> 00:13:16.962
You facilitate movement and interaction
by using colors and warm materials.

00:13:17.004 --> 00:13:18.380
It helps regulate mood

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:22.760
and create a sense of comfort
by placing windows strategically

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:27.264
for daylight and views and even views
onto the cells of other inmates.

00:13:27.348 --> 00:13:29.725
You reduce stress
and improve mental health.

00:13:29.725 --> 00:13:33.854
They can track the days and the seasons,
and it gives the inmates

00:13:33.854 --> 00:13:46.534
a psychological connection
to the outside world

00:13:46.617 --> 00:13:48.828
in this very constrained environment.

00:13:48.828 --> 00:13:53.082
The prisoner actually built a very
intimate relationship with their space,

00:13:53.165 --> 00:13:55.960
and it can either facility growth

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:58.754
or lead to a complete decline.

00:13:58.754 --> 00:14:03.968
And beyond this, I think our approach to
prisons says a lot about us as a society.

00:14:04.218 --> 00:14:07.179
Do we believe in the potential
for change in every person?

00:14:07.179 --> 00:14:11.475
Is there a room for dignity
and healing and redemption,

00:14:11.559 --> 00:14:14.144
even for the worst criminals?

00:14:14.144 --> 00:14:16.272
What do you guys think?
Let me know in the comments.

